WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:13.040
[Music]

00:00:13.120 --> 00:00:16.880
[Dr. Alan Guttmacher:] I'm Alan Guttmacher,
an obstetrician/gynecologist.

00:00:16.890 --> 00:00:24.710
I feel very strongly that a man trained in
modern medicine cannot carry out

00:00:24.710 --> 00:00:35.230
the high quality of performance because of
the restrictive, restrictions imposed

00:00:35.230 --> 00:00:40.870
by our very archaic abortion laws, and I feel
they must be changed.

00:00:40.870 --> 00:00:43.780
[Dr. Frank Ayd:] I'm Dr. Frank Ayd, a psychiatrist.

00:00:43.780 --> 00:00:48.449
I am opposed to any liberalization of abortion
laws,

00:00:48.449 --> 00:00:52.540
especially for psychiatric, social, and economic
indications.

00:00:52.540 --> 00:00:56.250
[Richard Lamm:] My name is Richard Lamm.

00:00:56.250 --> 00:00:58.760
By vocation I'm an attorney.

00:00:58.760 --> 00:01:02.829
By avocation I'm a member of the Colorado
House of Representatives.

00:01:02.829 --> 00:01:05.630
I was chief sponsor two years ago of Colorado's

00:01:05.630 --> 00:01:11.320
new liberalized abortion law, and I will act
today as the moderator.

00:01:11.440 --> 00:01:26.300
[Music]

00:01:26.540 --> 00:01:30.520
[Narrator:] Welcome to the exploration of
concepts and controversies in modern medicine,

00:01:30.520 --> 00:01:35.700
one of a series of programs dedicated to examining
the uncertain,

00:01:35.700 --> 00:01:40.820
candidly recognizing that much of today's
teaching is necessarily based upon opinion,

00:01:40.820 --> 00:01:45.180
and that the opinions of eminent physicians
in a given field vary widely.

00:01:45.180 --> 00:01:48.619
The National Medical Audiovisual Center believes
that openly airing

00:01:48.619 --> 00:01:54.540
such opposing views is a basic responsibility
of medical communications.

00:01:54.540 --> 00:01:57.689
The Honorable Richard Lamm, state representative,
state of Colorado,

00:01:57.689 --> 00:02:00.310
will act as moderator of this presentation.

00:02:00.310 --> 00:02:05.189
[Mr. Lamm:] I think one of the problems dealing
with the subject of abortion,

00:02:05.189 --> 00:02:08.280
and I think this is at least one thing that
we can all agree on,

00:02:08.280 --> 00:02:14.290
is the problem about in what category, in
what terms, and what discipline

00:02:14.290 --> 00:02:17.120
should the subject be discussed in?

00:02:17.120 --> 00:02:21.019
Some people say this is a strictly medical
problem.

00:02:21.019 --> 00:02:24.670
Other people I've heard say it's strictly
a legal problem.

00:02:24.670 --> 00:02:28.230
Many people are beginning to think it's perhaps
a sociological problem,

00:02:28.230 --> 00:02:30.379
a public health problem.

00:02:30.379 --> 00:02:36.900
There are many disciplines which lay claim
to an interest in the subject of abortion.

00:02:36.900 --> 00:02:39.819
I think that there is an agreement even among
a lot of people

00:02:39.819 --> 00:02:43.890
that it may be a little bit of all of these
disciplines, but there again

00:02:43.890 --> 00:02:48.180
that doesn't solve the problem because then
each profession continues to say,

00:02:48.180 --> 00:02:54.659
well, it's more of my problem than it is of
those other disciplines.

00:02:54.659 --> 00:03:00.830
Without solving that problem, let me give
you just a brief summary of what happens

00:03:00.830 --> 00:03:06.099
in a state that does liberalize its abortion
law.

00:03:06.099 --> 00:03:08.640
Colorado liberalized their abortion law by,

00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:12.190
it was signed by the governor on April 25th,
1967.

00:03:12.190 --> 00:03:16.690
We are a state of two and a half million people

00:03:16.690 --> 00:03:24.690
and there is approximately 35,000 live births
a year in Colorado.

00:03:24.690 --> 00:03:27.739
Previous to the signature of the new law there
was approximately

00:03:27.739 --> 00:03:34.129
50 abortions, hospital abortions performed
in Colorado in a year.

00:03:34.129 --> 00:03:40.900
In the first nine months after the passage
of our law, there was 140 abortions

00:03:40.900 --> 00:03:46.340
performed in Colorado hospitals, and in the
calendar year 1968 there was

00:03:46.340 --> 00:03:50.890
476 abortions performed.

00:03:50.890 --> 00:03:54.190
The grounds of these, that total 616 abortions,

00:03:54.190 --> 00:04:03.090
the grounds were about 55 percent under the
mental health provision of our law.

00:04:03.090 --> 00:04:09.970
There was approximately 26 percent of these
abortions were performed on non-residents

00:04:09.970 --> 00:04:17.810
of the state of Colorado which generally is
about, approximates the other medical care,

00:04:17.810 --> 00:04:24.450
being a state like Colorado drains a great
deal of western Kansas, eastern Utah.

00:04:24.450 --> 00:04:29.660
We have, uh, the abortions were performed
on, generally on people

00:04:29.660 --> 00:04:33.420
that make up a cross-section of our community.

00:04:33.420 --> 00:04:38.860
One of the things that was seen was is that
the lower economic groups

00:04:38.860 --> 00:04:43.820
have now had a lot more access to abortion than they had ever had under the previous law.

00:04:45.600 --> 00:04:47.700
Fifty percent of the abortions were performed

00:04:47.700 --> 00:04:53.990
on single women, 40 percent were performed on married women, and 10 percent on divorced women.

00:04:54.990 --> 00:05:00.380
Now, to try to discuss what problems there
are in the subject of abortion,

00:05:00.380 --> 00:05:05.780
to try to decide whether this is the right,
whether this is the end product,

00:05:05.780 --> 00:05:11.490
whether Colorado's experience is the end result
or whether or not it has gone too far,

00:05:11.490 --> 00:05:16.340
or whether or not we in effect have not gone
far enough, we will have other panelists,

00:05:16.340 --> 00:05:19.060
and we will turn to Dr. Guttmacher.

00:05:19.060 --> 00:05:25.640
[Dr. Guttmacher:] I think that the medical
audience realizes that

00:05:25.640 --> 00:05:31.970
there are seven additional states out of our
50 that have liberalized their laws,

00:05:31.970 --> 00:05:36.870
from my point of view not broadly enough,
but nevertheless eight of our states

00:05:36.870 --> 00:05:40.530
out of 50 now have more liberal laws.

00:05:40.530 --> 00:05:42.960
The old law of course in practically all states

00:05:42.960 --> 00:05:47.550
was that abortion could be done purely and
simply and only to preserve

00:05:47.550 --> 00:05:54.810
the life of the mother, and from my point
of view that is much too restrictive.

00:05:54.810 --> 00:06:00.750
You must remember that when these laws were
framed, medicine was a very different thing.

00:06:00.750 --> 00:06:06.090
Most of the laws date from about the 1820s
and '30s.

00:06:06.090 --> 00:06:12.040
At this time there was no anesthesia, there
was no antisepsis, there was no transfusion,

00:06:12.200 --> 00:06:18.860
and carrying out an abortion must have been a hideously painful,

00:06:19.000 --> 00:06:20.940
and I should think a dangerous procedure.

00:06:20.940 --> 00:06:26.240
Now, since then medicine has made magnificent
progress,

00:06:26.240 --> 00:06:33.110
and abortion in hospital can be performed
with extraordinary safety.

00:06:33.110 --> 00:06:39.030
I know in my rich experience, which goes back
in medicine some 40 years,

00:06:39.030 --> 00:06:46.880
I've never seen a death after therapeutic
abortion, and I think that it's fair to say

00:06:46.880 --> 00:06:50.080
that it is one of the least dangerous operations.

00:06:50.080 --> 00:06:54.600
Actually we have excellent statistics from
Czechoslovakia, from Hungary,

00:06:54.600 --> 00:07:00.030
on a million consecutive legal abortions,
and there were 25 deaths.

00:07:00.030 --> 00:07:03.770
That's 2.5 per hundred thousand operations.

00:07:03.770 --> 00:07:07.880
So I think we can say that this is a very
safe operation.

00:07:07.880 --> 00:07:11.330
Now, in addition to its safety,

00:07:11.330 --> 00:07:18.670
medicine now, I think, can pay much more attention to the whole personality of the individual.

00:07:18.670 --> 00:07:24.600
We can relax because most of our killers have
disappeared:

00:07:24.600 --> 00:07:32.910
pneumonia, various infections, and we can
now become more than just kind of carpenters.

00:07:32.910 --> 00:07:36.150
I think we can see the personality's totality,

00:07:36.150 --> 00:07:40.590
and from my point of view, abortion can contribute
tremendously

00:07:40.590 --> 00:07:46.500
to the happiness of the woman involved, and
very often to the family constellation.

00:07:46.500 --> 00:07:51.640
[Mr. Lamm:] To give his viewpoint on the subject,
Dr. Ayd.

00:07:51.640 --> 00:07:54.030
[Dr. Ayd:] Well, I think several points

00:07:54.030 --> 00:07:58.950
have already been made which warrant thorough
discussion.

00:07:58.950 --> 00:08:04.840
My first point would be of course that abortion
means that a human life is being taken.

00:08:04.840 --> 00:08:10.550
Now to declare that any individual's life,
at any time from the moment of conception,

00:08:10.550 --> 00:08:14.240
is devoid of value in my opinion is a judgment
that no man

00:08:14.240 --> 00:08:20.550
has the right to make, and to end an individual's
life is murder,

00:08:20.550 --> 00:08:26.370
committed because it has been decided that he's no longer a human being i

00:08:26.370 --> 00:08:31.540
in need of help and protection, but merely an object whose worth is measured according to

00:08:31.540 --> 00:08:38.040
whether or not his continued existence or
his destruction is expedient for someone or the nation.

00:08:38.720 --> 00:08:42.970
As Honorable Lamm has pointed out, whenever an abortion law

00:08:42.970 --> 00:08:48.420
is liberalized, there is an immediate and
significant increase in the number of

00:08:48.420 --> 00:08:53.590
therapeutic abortions performed, but I think
also what must be stressed,

00:08:53.590 --> 00:08:58.690
that regardless of how many legal abortions
are performed, there's absolutely

00:08:58.690 --> 00:09:04.390
no evidence that this in any way affects the
rate of illegal abortions.

00:09:04.390 --> 00:09:11.030
Furthermore, Dr. Guttmacher has pointed out
the progress in medicine and quite properly

00:09:11.030 --> 00:09:15.980
stressed the advances that have been made
which make surgery in general

00:09:15.980 --> 00:09:21.720
much safer than it used to be, but I honestly
believe that also it must be stressed

00:09:21.720 --> 00:09:26.970
that therapeutic abortion is, as the British
Medical Journal just simply

00:09:26.970 --> 00:09:34.170
pointed out on December 28, 1968 in an editorial,
it is not a simple operation.

00:09:34.170 --> 00:09:36.760
It does carry its hazards.

00:09:36.760 --> 00:09:39.010
Its safety depends on the surgeon performing

00:09:39.010 --> 00:09:43.230
the operation and even the method of termination
that is employed.

00:09:43.230 --> 00:09:48.500
Admittedly, Dr. Guttmacher's absolutely right
when he says that the mortality rate

00:09:48.500 --> 00:09:55.610
associated with therapeutic abortion is relatively
low, but the morbidity rate

00:09:55.610 --> 00:10:00.730
depends on the skill of the surgeon performing
the termination of pregnancy

00:10:00.730 --> 00:10:05.090
and also on the method of termination.

00:10:05.090 --> 00:10:07.310
Accurate statistics coming out of

00:10:07.310 --> 00:10:11.330
England for example, where very fine records
are kept, would indicate

00:10:11.330 --> 00:10:16.940
that the morbidity rate varies from one to
five percent,

00:10:16.940 --> 00:10:22.960
depending primarily on the method of termination
used, but I would point out

00:10:22.960 --> 00:10:28.150
that you do have hemorrhage, you do have perforation
of the womb,

00:10:28.150 --> 00:10:34.030
sometimes requiring secondary exploratory
abdominal surgery as a result,

00:10:34.030 --> 00:10:36.710
so that it's not an innocuous operation.

00:10:36.710 --> 00:10:43.230
But also as a psychiatrist I would stress
one very, very important aspect of this problem.

00:10:43.230 --> 00:10:50.510
Technically it is true, we can empty the womb
of the baby, but we cannot scrape the idea

00:10:50.510 --> 00:10:56.810
of the baby out of the mother's mind, and
therefore there are going to be women

00:10:56.810 --> 00:11:01.600
who while in the early stages of pregnancy
and being emotionally upset

00:11:01.600 --> 00:11:05.600
would be inclined to destroy the infant within their womb,

00:11:05.720 --> 00:11:12.120
subsequently, although the infant has been
removed by whatever termination method is used,

00:11:12.120 --> 00:11:18.470
the idea of having had a baby in her womb
is still in her mind, and that's not easily eradicated,

00:11:18.470 --> 00:11:23.900
and subsequent feelings of guilt
and things of this sort are real.

00:11:23.900 --> 00:11:28.250
We psychiatrists see them and we have to contend
with them.

00:11:28.250 --> 00:11:34.300
[Dr. Guttmacher:] Dick, I don't know whether
I'm allowed to to question some of the things Frank said,

00:11:34.980 --> 00:11:38.100
but we're old friends, so I think he won't
mind.

00:11:38.100 --> 00:11:41.890
In the first place, to him abortion is murder.

00:11:41.890 --> 00:11:45.310
I'm no murderer, and I've done abortions.

00:11:45.310 --> 00:11:51.780
To me it's quite different than shooting even
a man in warfare.

00:11:51.780 --> 00:11:59.290
To me these are undifferentiated cells which
have the potential for being a human being,

00:11:59.290 --> 00:12:08.340
but to me this baby is not a human being until born, and I am not willing to let it go that all doctors agree

00:12:08.480 --> 00:12:13.230
that this is murder because I daresay of the
group listening to me, or to us,

00:12:13.230 --> 00:12:18.290
a certain proportion, perhaps I think the
largest number, will think it's not murder.

00:12:18.290 --> 00:12:24.090
So I think you have to define this process according
to your own intellectual point of view,

00:12:24.090 --> 00:12:28.490
and perhaps your own social and religious
background,

00:12:28.490 --> 00:12:36.460
and in the second place, I think that the
psychiatric sequelae of abortion...

00:12:36.460 --> 00:12:38.130
this can be challenged.

00:12:38.130 --> 00:12:41.350
This is Dr. Ayd's experience.

00:12:41.350 --> 00:12:48.540
I daresay that he has perhaps had patients
who, perhaps because of religious affiliation,

00:12:48.540 --> 00:12:52.850
who have more feelings of guilt than perhaps most of the rest of us.

00:12:52.850 --> 00:12:58.600
Now we did a study at Mount Sinai, on our
ward patients who are largely Puerto Rican,

00:12:58.600 --> 00:13:06.820
and Marcus and Peck published a study on 50
consecutive abortions done at Mount Sinai,

00:13:06.820 --> 00:13:09.900
therapeutically for psychiatric indications.

00:13:09.900 --> 00:13:12.460
We had one patient of the 50

00:13:12.460 --> 00:13:16.440
who developed an acute psychosis about three
months post-abortum.

00:13:16.440 --> 00:13:20.050
This lasted about three months and apparently
she made a satisfactory recovery.

00:13:20.050 --> 00:13:26.630
As far as they could determine, and perhaps
their study was not as

00:13:26.630 --> 00:13:34.370
profound as perhaps Dr. Ayd would like, it they could
find no evidence of psychiatric sequelae.

00:13:34.370 --> 00:13:42.140
So that I think we, there are areas of disagreement, this thing is not nearly

00:13:42.140 --> 00:13:47.980
so easy to analyze as perhaps Dr. Ayd and
I both make it.

00:13:47.980 --> 00:13:52.360
I see it from one point of view and Dr. Ayd
sees it from an entirely different point of view.

00:13:52.700 --> 00:13:54.920
[Mr. Lamm:] I would like to ask you both

00:13:54.920 --> 00:13:57.820
what you would write into the law if you were
dealing,

00:13:57.820 --> 00:14:00.940
what kind of law would you propose to deal
with the problem of abortion?

00:14:00.940 --> 00:14:03.089
[Dr. Ayd:] Well, before I answer your question,
sir,

00:14:03.089 --> 00:14:08.200
I think that in order to keep this dialogue
going in a meaningful way for the audience

00:14:08.200 --> 00:14:12.870
that I should make two comments about what
Alan has said.

00:14:12.870 --> 00:14:17.590
Number one, this study that he referred to
of only 50 patients,

00:14:17.590 --> 00:14:22.130
I'd have to have more data as a scientist before I would accept or reject

00:14:22.130 --> 00:14:24.770
the validity of the findings.

00:14:24.770 --> 00:14:28.440
Number two, although it is true, I am a Roman
Catholic,

00:14:28.440 --> 00:14:32.520
I'm not opposed to abortion just on religious
grounds.

00:14:32.520 --> 00:14:36.250
In fact, the majority of the patients that
I'm called on to treat

00:14:36.250 --> 00:14:41.730
are not Catholic, and still I see feelings of
guilt and so forth.

00:14:41.730 --> 00:14:49.020
But, he also raised I think one of the crucial
questions and that is, is the fetus a human being?

00:14:49.360 --> 00:14:53.420
Now I admit that many abortion supporters
say that the fetus

00:14:53.420 --> 00:14:58.150
is nothing more than a blob of protoplasm,
nothing more than that,

00:14:58.150 --> 00:15:03.120
but I don't think that they honestly recognize
their own contradiction.

00:15:03.260 --> 00:15:09.200
Now, if the fetus is not a human being, what
is it that's damaged in the early weeks

00:15:09.210 --> 00:15:14.040
of its experience by a viral infection such
as rubella, by drugs,

00:15:14.040 --> 00:15:18.480
chemicals, x-rays and so forth, which is used to justify abortion?

00:15:18.480 --> 00:15:25.060
If the fetus is not a human being, why are
so many physicians and scientists

00:15:25.060 --> 00:15:32.190
spending millions of dollars and countless
hours trying to develop a vaccine for rubella,

00:15:32.190 --> 00:15:38.839
trying to devise ways of avoiding birth defects,
trying to develop an artificial placenta,

00:15:38.839 --> 00:15:45.050
trying to fertilize the ovum outside the body
in a test tube

00:15:45.050 --> 00:15:49.529
and by artificial insemination and genetic
engineering and genetic surgery,

00:15:49.529 --> 00:15:57.620
to enable man to improve the mere and remote descendants that he may have.

00:15:57.620 --> 00:16:01.990
Now furthermore, in this country, if the fetus is not a human being,

00:16:01.990 --> 00:16:07.100
then I must ask the question, why does the
United States Food and Drug Administration

00:16:07.100 --> 00:16:08.290
caution against,

00:16:08.290 --> 00:16:14.000
or in some instances prohibit, the administration
of certain drugs in the first three months

00:16:14.000 --> 00:16:15.000
of pregnancy?

00:16:15.000 --> 00:16:16.510
[Dr. Guttmacher:] Now I can answer that, because...

00:16:16.510 --> 00:16:17.930
[Dr. Ayd:] May I finish please?

00:16:17.930 --> 00:16:18.930
[Dr. Guttmacher:] I thought you had.

00:16:18.930 --> 00:16:25.940
[Dr. Ayd:] No, I have one other point that
I'd like to make and that is, uh,

00:16:26.080 --> 00:16:29.640
it, to my mind, to say that a fetus is not human is sheer nonsense.

00:16:29.640 --> 00:16:33.660
Dr. Guttmacher was a fetus, I was a fetus,
you were a fetus.

00:16:33.660 --> 00:16:38.721
You see, our existence began this way, and
for someone to terminate it,

00:16:38.721 --> 00:16:41.950
if someone had terminated the fetal existence
of any one of the three of us,

00:16:41.950 --> 00:16:43.980
we wouldn't be here on this panel today.

00:16:43.980 --> 00:16:46.911
So that the fetus is a human being.

00:16:46.911 --> 00:16:50.060
I think it's semantics to say that human life
doesn't begin

00:16:50.060 --> 00:16:56.540
until implantation or until the third month or the sixth month or until a baby's born.

00:16:56.540 --> 00:17:00.360
What constitutes humaneness or humanness?

00:17:00.420 --> 00:17:04.280
[Dr. Guttmacher:] Well, I agree with you for
once, about this:

00:17:04.280 --> 00:17:11.480
I think that a sperm is a living potential human being, I think an egg is a potential living human being.

00:17:11.580 --> 00:17:15.400
I see nothing that is changed by the process of fertilization.

00:17:15.400 --> 00:17:21.040
These were living cells before fertilization,
they become, continue to live after fertilization.

00:17:21.040 --> 00:17:23.360
But they are potential human beings.

00:17:23.360 --> 00:17:24.460
This is where you and I differ.

00:17:24.460 --> 00:17:27.890
I think that human life, potential human life

00:17:27.890 --> 00:17:30.090
begins at the moment of fertilization.

00:17:30.090 --> 00:17:32.650
It actually precedes it.

00:17:32.650 --> 00:17:39.080
Now what I think is that you're not, I was
a fetus to be sure, but I was a potential human being

00:17:39.260 --> 00:17:42.870
until I was born, and that's where you and
I differ, and no matter how much

00:17:42.870 --> 00:17:45.860
we argue we're not coming to a common area
of agreement.

00:17:45.860 --> 00:17:47.590
[Dr. Ayd:] Well, I'll agree that it would
be

00:17:47.590 --> 00:17:50.230
very difficult to come to a common area of
agreement.

00:17:50.230 --> 00:17:51.890
I will agree that the sperm

00:17:51.890 --> 00:17:56.679
is a living cell and so too is an ovum, but
the genetic composition of a sperm

00:17:56.679 --> 00:18:00.170
is different from the genetic composition
of the ovum,

00:18:00.170 --> 00:18:05.550
and it isn't until you have fertilization that you now have a unique human being

00:18:05.550 --> 00:18:10.550
that never existed before and will never exist again and after all, the ovum only contributed half

00:18:10.550 --> 00:18:13.309
of a genetic or chromosomal makeup of that
fertilized...

00:18:13.309 --> 00:18:14.309
[Dr. Guttmacher:] But that is a unique cell,
too.

00:18:14.309 --> 00:18:15.309
[Dr. Ayd:] No, no, it's not though.

00:18:15.309 --> 00:18:16.580
[Dr. Guttmacher:] The sperm is a unique cell.

00:18:16.580 --> 00:18:20.230
Every sperm has a different chromosomal makeup.

00:18:20.230 --> 00:18:21.400
[Dr. Ayd:] Precisely.

00:18:21.400 --> 00:18:24.730
But it only has half of any other cell in
the body

00:18:24.730 --> 00:18:28.240
and so does the ovum, but when you have a
new human being,

00:18:28.240 --> 00:18:32.660
every cell, until they're differentiated again
into ovum or sperm,

00:18:32.660 --> 00:18:35.020
has a complete chromosomal complement, not
a half.

00:18:35.020 --> 00:18:42.130
Now, I mean, to say that this sperm is a potential
human being, I think genetically at least

00:18:42.130 --> 00:18:46.460
you're completely erroneous, and that it is
misleading to say this...

00:18:46.460 --> 00:18:50.870
[Dr. Guttmacher:] You have half a human being
then, you'll grant that?

00:18:50.870 --> 00:18:58.160
[Dr. Ayd:] No, because it can become nothing but sperm until it actually fertilizes an ovum,

00:18:59.160 --> 00:19:01.680
then you have a new human being.

00:19:01.680 --> 00:19:02.730
[Dr. Guttmacher:] I think we're splitting
hairs.

00:19:02.730 --> 00:19:06.900
You and I differ in the fact that I think
that a fetus is until birth is a potential,

00:19:06.900 --> 00:19:11.270
you think a fetus is actual as a human being
immediately after fertilization,

00:19:11.270 --> 00:19:13.400
so let's agree on that and let's go on.

00:19:13.400 --> 00:19:14.400
[Dr. Ayd:] All right, fine.

00:19:14.400 --> 00:19:15.790
[Dr. Guttmacher:] We're losing a lot of valuable
time.

00:19:15.790 --> 00:19:22.429
[Mr. Lamm:] I would then be interested, both
doctors, to, uh, at your viewpoints on what

00:19:22.429 --> 00:19:23.900
should be the indications

00:19:23.900 --> 00:19:27.450
of therapeutic abortion when you're dealing
in the realm of law,

00:19:27.450 --> 00:19:33.850
and that to me is the problem right now, is because medical indications

00:19:33.850 --> 00:19:38.200
only designate a certain stage of medical learning at a certain point in history

00:19:38.200 --> 00:19:42.430
and yet when you try to write these things
into law, you have at least in Colorado,

00:19:42.430 --> 00:19:48.240
one hundred legislators, only one
of whom is a doctor, trying to decide

00:19:48.240 --> 00:19:51.050
what is good medical practice, and this is
very difficult.

00:19:51.050 --> 00:19:55.980
I would like to ask you, what would you see
to be a model abortion law, or dealing with the subject?

00:19:56.120 --> 00:20:00.530
[Dr. Ayd:] Well, in dealing with the subject
my answer immediately would be that I live

00:20:00.530 --> 00:20:02.370
in a pluralistic society.

00:20:02.370 --> 00:20:06.370
I'm perfectly willing to accept the law that
is now on the books,

00:20:06.370 --> 00:20:09.549
which permits abortion to save the life of
the mother.

00:20:09.549 --> 00:20:15.080
I frankly, at this moment, deny that there
are any psychiatric indications

00:20:15.180 --> 00:20:18.100
that justify liberalization of the abortion laws.

00:20:18.100 --> 00:20:20.340
After all, psychiatry has made progress,

00:20:20.340 --> 00:20:25.300
just as surgery and medicine has made, and
it would be a rare pregnant woman,

00:20:25.300 --> 00:20:29.540
psychiatrically ill, who could not be carried
to term safely

00:20:29.540 --> 00:20:33.290
with the modern psychiatric therapies that
we have.

00:20:33.290 --> 00:20:36.880
Now, once you get out of the psychiatry and the medical indications,

00:20:37.080 --> 00:20:41.080
you're talking in terms of social and economic indications.

00:20:41.090 --> 00:20:47.220
If we're going to have abortions done for
social and economic reasons,

00:20:47.220 --> 00:20:53.900
then I think we're in for very dangerous waters
and as a matter of fact,

00:20:53.900 --> 00:20:58.310
I confess that most of the so-called psychiatric indications which have

00:20:58.310 --> 00:21:03.260
justified up to 70 percent of the abortions
done in your state, and in fact in California,

00:21:03.260 --> 00:21:07.020
and even in the state of Maryland from which I come, have been done

00:21:07.100 --> 00:21:11.280
for alleged psychiatric reasons, they're called reactive depressions.

00:21:11.280 --> 00:21:12.280
Until the laws were liberalized,

00:21:12.280 --> 00:21:16.240
I'll tell you, we never had so many reactive depressions, all right?

00:21:16.240 --> 00:21:22.660
Now in fact these are socio-economic indications, not strictly speaking,

00:21:22.780 --> 00:21:24.760
psychiatric indications.

00:21:24.760 --> 00:21:28.480
Now what I also must stress is that

00:21:28.480 --> 00:21:34.990
the evidence we already have from the United
States and from England is that

00:21:34.990 --> 00:21:40.710
the minute you liberalize the abortion laws,
women think that they are entitled,

00:21:40.710 --> 00:21:45.500
that they have a legal right to abortion on
demand, and this puts pressure

00:21:45.600 --> 00:21:48.580
on the physician and I'll illustrate that.

00:21:48.580 --> 00:21:50.250
Suppose the physician prescribes

00:21:50.250 --> 00:21:55.340
an oral contraceptive or inserts an intrauterine
device and this woman becomes pregnant.

00:21:55.340 --> 00:21:59.450
Now she comes back and accuses him and says,
"You're responsible for this.

00:21:59.450 --> 00:22:00.960
Do something about it."

00:22:00.960 --> 00:22:05.210
These emotional pressures are going to be
to abort this woman.

00:22:05.210 --> 00:22:09.620
Now also there's been something that was unforeseen
when the laws

00:22:09.620 --> 00:22:14.490
were liberalized in Colorado and in California
and in Maryland, and that is

00:22:14.490 --> 00:22:20.360
that we now have an unwritten, workable rule which goes, "If in doubt, abort,"

00:22:20.360 --> 00:22:24.429
because if one allows the pregnancy to go
on, this may subsequently be used

00:22:24.429 --> 00:22:27.780
as grounds for a malpractice suit against
the physician.

00:22:27.780 --> 00:22:31.320
If I may go back from the socio-economic,

00:22:31.460 --> 00:22:34.440
and still take up the other
indication, namely the question of

00:22:34.440 --> 00:22:36.370
a suspected fetal abnormality.

00:22:36.370 --> 00:22:39.620
This puts the physician in a very difficult
position

00:22:39.620 --> 00:22:45.180
because he must now decide how many normals
he's going to sacrifice

00:22:45.180 --> 00:22:47.950
to prevent the birth of one abnormal baby.

00:22:47.950 --> 00:22:50.309
Now this is one reason why physicians

00:22:50.309 --> 00:22:57.929
in general are not too in favor of abortion.They find it repugnant, and if you

00:22:57.929 --> 00:23:02.910
actually check the figures in your state and
in California and in Maryland,

00:23:02.910 --> 00:23:08.460
what is coming out is that the majority of
the abortions are being done in a few hospitals

00:23:08.460 --> 00:23:11.780
by a relatively small number of physicians.

00:23:11.780 --> 00:23:14.620
The majority of obstetrician-gynecologists

00:23:14.620 --> 00:23:18.590
are not performing abortions any more than
they did before the law

00:23:18.590 --> 00:23:22.800
was liberalized, and a great majority of them are doing them for what would

00:23:22.800 --> 00:23:24.670
still be considered valid medical indications.

00:23:24.670 --> 00:23:26.910
[Mr. Lamm:] Turn to Dr. Guttmacher.

00:23:26.910 --> 00:23:29.410
[Dr. Guttmacher:] I think we must liberalize
laws

00:23:29.410 --> 00:23:32.460
because the current situation is highly discriminatory,

00:23:32.460 --> 00:23:35.530
both in regard to legal and illegal abortion.

00:23:35.530 --> 00:23:41.860
In New York there are five times as many therapeutic
abortions done on the private services

00:23:41.860 --> 00:23:45.060
of hospitals than on the clinic services.

00:23:45.200 --> 00:23:46.671
Theoretically then, the private patient

00:23:46.671 --> 00:23:47.910
is five times as ill.

00:23:47.910 --> 00:23:48.910
This is ridiculous.

00:23:48.910 --> 00:23:51.930
In the second place, anybody knows that

00:23:51.930 --> 00:23:57.540
$700 will buy a safe abortion in Puerto Rico,
and if you don't have $700

00:23:57.540 --> 00:24:02.700
and you only have five bucks, your chance
of having a safe abortion is extraordinarily hideous.

00:24:03.180 --> 00:24:07.680
We have a million illegal abortions estimated
in the United States,

00:24:07.680 --> 00:24:12.900
and what I want to see is to give poor people,
people who don't have the money

00:24:12.900 --> 00:24:18.510
and the influence a decent break, and I think we have to do this by liberalizing our laws

00:24:18.510 --> 00:24:23.660
and spelling out for the time being
what are those indications quite clearly.

00:24:23.660 --> 00:24:27.950
Now, I agree that abortion should be done
for the preservation

00:24:27.950 --> 00:24:31.370
of life or health, mental or physical.

00:24:31.370 --> 00:24:33.400
I agree that abortion should be done

00:24:33.400 --> 00:24:36.309
in cases of proved sex crime.

00:24:36.309 --> 00:24:39.419
I agree that abortion should be done when
there is

00:24:39.419 --> 00:24:44.930
grave likelihood of the fetus being born either
malformed or retarded.

00:24:44.930 --> 00:24:50.390
Furthermore, I think that any woman who has
had four living children should have the choice

00:24:50.390 --> 00:24:55.630
as to whether or not another pregnancy is
gonna be an asset to the family constellation.

00:24:55.630 --> 00:24:57.299
Who can make this judgment better?

00:24:57.299 --> 00:25:01.080
A doctor, a lawyer, a legislator?

00:25:01.080 --> 00:25:02.080
No!

00:25:02.080 --> 00:25:03.950
The woman herself is in the position to make
that decision.

00:25:03.950 --> 00:25:07.400
I further feel that any woman who's 40 years old should have the choice

00:25:07.440 --> 00:25:09.540
as to whether or not to remain pregnant.

00:25:09.540 --> 00:25:13.640
I agree with Plato in his Republic, who thought that any woman of 40 or more

00:25:13.640 --> 00:25:17.510
should be aborted and by that time she certainly
is mature enough

00:25:17.510 --> 00:25:19.390
to make the proper decision.

00:25:19.390 --> 00:25:22.120
I think that any woman who is physically

00:25:22.120 --> 00:25:27.020
or mentally incapable of caring for a child
should be aborted if she wishes it,

00:25:27.020 --> 00:25:31.830
and I think that any girl of 16 or less who
is illegitimately pregnant

00:25:31.830 --> 00:25:36.740
should be aborted if she desires it, because I can't see that that's a constructive experience

00:25:36.740 --> 00:25:40.700
for either the girl or a healthy environment
for the child to be born in.

00:25:40.700 --> 00:25:43.150
[Mr. Lamm:] Dr. Guttmacher, let me ask you
this: Do you think that the fact

00:25:43.150 --> 00:25:46.800
of the statistics that were just presented by
Dr. Ayd with regards to the large number

00:25:46.800 --> 00:25:50.429
of abortions performed under psychiatric
grounds, do you think number one,

00:25:50.429 --> 00:25:53.340
that this needs explanation, and if you do
would you care to comment?

00:25:53.340 --> 00:25:58.550
[Dr. Guttmacher:] No, I think it simply shows
a sense of humanity in the medical profession.

00:25:58.550 --> 00:26:02.400
We're not rigid psychiatrists, we're not saying
a woman's going crazy because

00:26:02.400 --> 00:26:05.580
she's not aborted, we're simply saying that
this woman

00:26:05.580 --> 00:26:10.210
doesn't have the emotional maturity, the emotional
background to be a good parent,

00:26:10.210 --> 00:26:14.220
or we feel that this pregnancy is going to
be emotionally destructive on her,

00:26:14.220 --> 00:26:18.290
something that's not going to add to her happiness
or to her growth

00:26:18.290 --> 00:26:21.400
or to her personality and therefore it can
be eliminated.

00:26:21.400 --> 00:26:23.100
We're looking at psychiatry

00:26:23.100 --> 00:26:28.460
not from the point of view of insanity and
sanity or psychoses, but looking at it

00:26:28.460 --> 00:26:30.560
from a much more general point of view.

00:26:30.560 --> 00:26:34.710
What is the growth likelihood of the

00:26:34.710 --> 00:26:36.890
personality due to the pregnancy?

00:26:36.890 --> 00:26:38.520
That to me is a much more important concept.

00:26:38.520 --> 00:26:40.049
[Mr. Lamm:] You have a rebuttal to that?

00:26:40.049 --> 00:26:42.410
[Dr. Ayd:] Yes, I would simply say that right
away we're putting

00:26:42.410 --> 00:26:47.100
some people into the classification of second-class citizens and that

00:26:47.100 --> 00:26:51.000
if someone is unwanted this is justification
for terminating their existence,

00:26:51.000 --> 00:26:56.780
and that if someone is likely to
be a burden either on the mother

00:26:56.780 --> 00:27:01.470
or on society, that this is justification
for terminating their existence.

00:27:01.470 --> 00:27:07.290
Now, my feeling is that if that philosophy,
which is an anti-life philosophy in my opinion,

00:27:07.290 --> 00:27:11.809
permeates the United States, then the weak
and the dependent and the aged

00:27:11.809 --> 00:27:16.820
have a lot to be afraid of, because if your
existence is going to depend on your quality,

00:27:16.820 --> 00:27:20.860
and not on the inviolateness of your life,

00:27:20.860 --> 00:27:25.610
not on its intrinsically determined value,
but just on that judgment

00:27:25.610 --> 00:27:30.150
as to whether or not you're gonna be a burden either to a mother

00:27:30.150 --> 00:27:34.660
or to society, then I think many, many people in the United States today

00:27:34.800 --> 00:27:37.000
better start worrying, because they're in
trouble.

00:27:37.010 --> 00:27:39.850
[Dr. Guttmacher:] I don't think that follows
logically at all,

00:27:39.850 --> 00:27:41.270
but go on with your...

00:27:41.270 --> 00:27:44.490
[Mr. Lamm:] Let me ask you, along the same
line though,

00:27:44.490 --> 00:27:48.390
we had evidence before the Colorado Legislature
that all we were doing was bringing up

00:27:48.390 --> 00:27:52.030
what was obviously a long-existing medical
practice,

00:27:52.030 --> 00:27:57.370
for instance in the 1960 issue of Obstetrics
and Gynecology, there was a statement

00:27:57.370 --> 00:28:00.980
showing the indications performed, abortions
were performed

00:28:00.980 --> 00:28:04.400
in the 367 major hospitals in the United States,

00:28:04.400 --> 00:28:08.440
and neuropsychiatric, rubella, all of these
reasons were already being performed,

00:28:08.440 --> 00:28:12.260
and so we thought all we were doing is bringing
up to law what medical practice

00:28:12.260 --> 00:28:13.390
had been doing for years.

00:28:13.390 --> 00:28:16.360
[Dr. Ayd:] Well that doesn't necessarily make
it right.

00:28:16.360 --> 00:28:20.300
I know this is exactly what you've done and
I have no question about

00:28:20.300 --> 00:28:26.919
the sincerity of your motives at all, but
I insist, you see, that we've got to decide

00:28:26.919 --> 00:28:30.490
whether or not human life is sacred, because
if human life isn't sacred

00:28:30.490 --> 00:28:32.150
nothing else is.

00:28:32.150 --> 00:28:35.299
We've got to decide whether you, or I, or
any other person

00:28:35.299 --> 00:28:39.450
in this world has the right to decide who
shall live and who shall die.

00:28:39.450 --> 00:28:42.679
[Mr. Lamm:] I think on that, I see that

00:28:42.679 --> 00:28:43.730
we are getting a summary.

00:28:43.730 --> 00:28:44.840
I wonder if each of you would like to give

00:28:44.840 --> 00:28:47.060
just a very short summary of your positions
again?

00:28:47.060 --> 00:28:49.870
[Dr. Ayd:] Well, I am opposed to abortion

00:28:49.870 --> 00:28:55.360
and I would stress that so far in England
and the United States,

00:28:55.360 --> 00:28:59.880
abortions have been done primarily for so-called
psychiatric indications

00:28:59.880 --> 00:29:04.260
on young, single girls, you see, who certainly are not

00:29:04.360 --> 00:29:07.760
socioeconomically classifiable as disadvantaged.

00:29:08.060 --> 00:29:09.060
[Mr. Lamm:] Dr. Guttmacher.

00:29:09.100 --> 00:29:11.860
[Dr. Guttmacher:] I would say that the main
difference

00:29:11.860 --> 00:29:14.590
is that I have just as much reverence for
human life, I believe,

00:29:14.590 --> 00:29:17.770
as anyone who opposes changes in the abortion laws.

00:29:17.770 --> 00:29:18.770
I want them changed

00:29:18.770 --> 00:29:20.770
because I revere human life.

00:29:20.770 --> 00:29:23.250
I want it to be strong and healthy and happy.

00:29:23.250 --> 00:29:26.539
[Mr. Lamm:] Let me say in summary that in
dealing with the problem,

00:29:26.539 --> 00:29:30.700
at least from the point of the legislator,
we have certain problems.

00:29:30.700 --> 00:29:32.400
Number one is, should abortion be performed?

00:29:32.400 --> 00:29:35.760
Number two, by who, where and with whose consent?

00:29:35.760 --> 00:29:37.730
These are some of the problems

00:29:37.730 --> 00:29:40.600
that we deal with in the legislature as opposed
to the medical.

00:29:40.600 --> 00:29:41.799
Thank you.

00:29:43.420 --> 00:29:46.880
[Narrator:] We thank Dr. Alan Guttmacher,
Dr. Frank Ayd Jr.,

00:29:46.890 --> 00:29:50.510
and the Honorable Richard Lamm for their interesting
analysis of a critical problem

00:29:50.510 --> 00:29:52.190
in patient care.

00:29:52.190 --> 00:29:55.230
In subsequent programs we shall continue to
record equally

00:29:55.230 --> 00:29:59.110
significant concepts and controversies in
modern medicine.

00:29:59.110 --> 00:30:02.950
The opinions expressed on this program do
not necessarily constitute endorsement

00:30:02.950 --> 00:30:09.620
by the Department of Health, Education, and Welfare, the Public Health Service, or its constitutents.

00:30:09.700 --> 00:30:35.300
[Music]

